---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageIntro from "@/components/PageIntro.astro";
import ServiceCard from "@/components/ServiceCard.astro";
import CTASection from "@/components/CTASection.astro";
import { breadcrumbSchema } from "@/lib/schema";
import { coreServices, relatedServices } from "@/data/services";

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Services", path: "/services/" },
];

const schemas = [breadcrumbSchema(breadcrumbs)];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Services | Basement Finishing & Remodeling | Hunting Tanner Construction"
  description="Residential construction services from Hunting Tanner Construction: full basement finishing, basement remodeling, bathrooms and bedrooms, wet bars, entertainment spaces, offices, and gyms across Utah County."
  path="/services/"
  pageType="service"
  schemas={schemas}
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Services"
    heading="What we build"
    lede="Hunting Tanner Construction specializes in basements today: finishing them, remodeling them, and building the rooms inside them, with a foundation designed to grow into broader residential construction."
    breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid-3">
        {coreServices.map((service) => (
          <ServiceCard service={service} showIncludes />
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Full scope detail lives here rather than on the homepage, where it
      crowded out the photography. Kept verbatim for SEO. */}
  <section class="section section--alt" id="whats-included">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">What's Included</span>
      <h2>What a Utah basement finish includes</h2>
      <p>
        A basement finish touches nearly every construction trade: framing,
        electrical, plumbing, HVAC, insulation, drywall, paint, doors, trim,
        and flooring, sequenced in the right order and inspected at the
        right stages. When those pieces are coordinated well, the project
        feels surprisingly calm. When they aren't, homeowners get the
        stories you've heard from neighbors.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Layout and space planning</strong>: bedroom placement,
          bathroom location relative to plumbing rough-ins, family-room
          sizing, and storage that's planned instead of leftover.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Permits and inspections</strong>: coordinated with your
          city's building department and scheduled into the timeline.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Framing and insulation</strong>: exterior wall furring,
          interior walls, soffits around mechanical runs, and sound
          insulation where it matters.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Electrical</strong>: lighting layout, outlets, smoke and
          CO detection, and circuits for how the space will actually be
          used.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plumbing and HVAC</strong>: bathroom completion from the
          rough-in, wet-bar supply and drain, and heating and cooling
          balanced for below-grade comfort.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Drywall, paint, doors, and trim</strong>: the finish
          stage where the space starts looking like the rest of your home.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Flooring and fixtures</strong>: carpet, LVP, or tile
          selected through organized allowances.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every project runs on the same structure:
        <a href="/process/">a six-step process</a> with a written scope, an
        itemized proposal, and change orders documented before any changed
        work proceeds.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Room-type detail — anchor targets for the service cards above */}
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Popular Spaces</span>
      <h2>The rooms Utah homeowners add most</h2>

      <h3 id="bathrooms-bedrooms">Basement bathrooms &amp; bedrooms</h3>
      <p>
        A conforming bedroom and a full bathroom are the two additions that
        change how a basement counts, for daily life and for how the home
        functions with guests, teenagers, or family staying long-term.
        Bedrooms need proper egress windows and smoke detection; bathrooms
        need supply, drain, and ventilation done right. Both get planned
        around your home's existing rough-ins and window locations.
      </p>

      <h3 id="family-rooms">Family &amp; entertainment rooms</h3>
      <p>
        The big open room is the heart of most basement projects: movie
        nights, game tables, toy space, hosting. Good ones are designed
        deliberately, with TV wall placement decided before the electrical
        goes in, lighting zones instead of one switch, sound control in the
        ceiling, and storage built into the plan.
      </p>

      <h3 id="wet-bars">Wet bars &amp; kitchenettes</h3>
      <p>
        A snack center with a sink, drink fridge, microwave shelf, and real
        cabinetry keeps popcorn nights downstairs, and makes the space feel
        finished in a way paint alone never does. Full kitchenettes can also
        support long-term guests. We'll talk honestly about which level fits
        your budget and how each affects plumbing scope.
      </p>

      <h3 id="offices-gyms">Home offices &amp; gyms</h3>
      <p>
        Basements make excellent offices and gyms for the same reasons:
        quiet, separation, and stable temperatures. Offices get planned for
        lighting quality and outlets where the desk actually goes. Gyms get
        durable flooring, mirrors, ventilation, and ceiling clearance
        checked before anything is built.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Cost drivers and timeline — moved off the homepage */}
  <section class="section section--alt" id="cost-and-timeline">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Cost &amp; Timeline</span>
      <h2>What basement finishing costs in Utah</h2>
      <p>
        Homeowners deserve a straight answer on pricing, and the honest one
        is that basements vary widely. The main drivers are:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Square footage</strong> being finished</li>
        <li><strong>Bathrooms</strong>: count, and whether a rough-in exists</li>
        <li><strong>Wet features</strong>: bars and kitchenettes add plumbing and cabinetry</li>
        <li><strong>Bedroom count</strong>: egress windows and closets</li>
        <li><strong>Finish level</strong>: flooring, tile, cabinetry, and built-in choices</li>
        <li><strong>Existing conditions</strong>: ceiling height, mechanical layout, moisture</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Rather than quoting a misleading per-square-foot number, we walk the
        space, write the scope, and price it line by line, so you can see
        exactly where the investment goes and adjust the plan before
        construction, not during it. If you share a target range early,
        we'll design the scope around it.
      </p>

      <h3>How long a basement finish takes</h3>
      <p>
        Most projects take anywhere from 6-10 weeks typically, depending on
        size, the number of wet areas, inspection scheduling, and selection
        lead times. Your proposal includes an estimated timeline for your
        specific scope, and weekly updates keep you current once work
        begins.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h2>Also part of the toolkit</h2>
      <ul>
        {relatedServices.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        Planning something residential that isn't a basement, like a future
        kitchen, a larger remodel, an addition? We're focused on basements
        while the company builds its track record, but we're happy to hear
        what you're planning and tell you honestly whether we're the right
        fit or can point you in a good direction.
        <a href="/contact/">Start the conversation</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Serving Utah County and Herriman</h2>
      <p>
        All services are available across our service area:
        <a href="/saratoga-springs-basement-finishing/">Saratoga Springs</a>,
        <a href="/lehi-basement-finishing/">Lehi</a>,
        <a href="/herriman-basement-finishing/">Herriman</a>, and
        <a href="/utah-county-basement-finishing/">communities throughout
        Utah County</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection withForm formId="services-form" />
</BaseLayout>
